[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hello, I\'m Albert Bosson. I teach animation at Curious Refuge, and today I\'m visiting the YouTube channel to show you one of my favorite styles in filmmaking – cartoon characters standing in real live-action footage.'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Mary Poppins, Roger Rabbit, Space Jam did it, and then the whole style kind of died down for a decade, at least in the mainstream, until Coyote vs. Acme finally brought it back, and the reaction to'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it shows that people still crave hybrid 2D- live-action filmmaking. But one of the reasons we haven\'t gotten more of this kind of distribution is that it\'s more difficult than'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'combining animation and live action, because every frame has to be planned and created twice. But it\'s become a lot easier lately, and you don\'t have to be a Hollywood studio to do it anymore. You can achieve this'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with a bit of C and a little After Effects, and I\'ll show you exactly how. And since we\'re aiming for professional results, I invited my cinematographer friend Joshua Borkot to film it.'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The most important recommendation I can give you when starting a project like this is organization. Before you shoot, have a detailed script and storyboard of every shot you want. Because you\'re actually doing it'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'twice. You shoot live action footage with your actor, in this case me , and create a bunch of 2D footage that you\'ll only see in post-production, even after the'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'editing is complete. The story I tell in this short film is that a 2D neighbor moves in next door and brings a pie just to say hello and chat. But'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things get heated when he pulls out a bottle of, you know, cartoon whiskey. I have a drink , my head explodes, then my cat comes in and really doesn\'t like the new'

[02:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'guest, and they start fighting, completely destroying my living room. One of my personal favorite platforms for accessing a wide range of AI tools in a single interface is magnific.com, but you'

[02:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can use any tool that has access to modern models, such as the Google Nano Banana family or C Dance. So it\'s up to you. For this project, we will need a full character spread of'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'each 2D character you want to integrate into your game footage. So, front, side and back views, and I also like to add a close-up of the face. All this on a'

[02:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'neutral gray background. The image model you use is, of course, up to you. You can see me trying out different options, like the C Dream, the Google Nano Banana 2, and the Nano Banana Pro, and eventually settling on this'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Beaver version. The final clue I ended up using was this: full spread of a 2D animated classic cartoon style, slightly humanoid, short, pot-'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bellied beaver with brown fur and a light brown belly, large eyes, giant deer teeth, large, wide and black beaver tail, pose A, front, side and back views, additional'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'close-up of only the face with a neutral expression, neutral grey background with hashtag #808080, no text. So, 808080 is a hexadecimal code that simply narrows down the exact gray tone.'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The model, Google Nano Banana 2, doesn\'t perfectly reproduce tones according to hexadecimal code, but it gets you close to them. And also, the reason I write in the A pose instead of the T pose is because I find that the'

[03:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A pose saves a little bit of space, you know, characters in the T pose have their arms very wide apart , and it\'s unnecessary. But I\'m not quite finished yet . You can see that there are duplicates of the side'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'poses. This can be moved here. There are better ways to organize this. So I\'m going to take this into Photoshop and clean it up a little bit. And here is the end result. I\'m also going to'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use the character\'s face-cropped rotation as a reference for the facial expression sheet and create a whole grid of different expressions based on the character\'s frontal view. Depending on your project, you'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can also do this with a side view, but in my case I\'m going to do it with just a front view. The tip I will use in this case, and you will have to adapt it to your own, is this: use the image alone'

[04:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solely as a style and reference for the character. Create a 3x6 grid expression sheet with a variety of beaver facial expressions. Use the same style and frontal perspective, and then again with a'

[04:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'neutral gray background. No text, no duplication of emotions. It wo n\'t be 100% accurate, so you run it a few times and then combine the expressions you like in Photoshop. And this will help maintain'

